Sure! Those numbers are already the latitude and longitude you’re looking for:

| Latitude | Longitude |
|----------|-----------|
| 37.4224764 | –122.0842499 |

If you’d like to confirm or retrieve additional information (like the formatted address) using the Google Geocoding API, you can perform a **reverse‑geocode** request with those coordinates. Here’s how you would do it:

```text
https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng=37.4224764,-122.0842499&key=YOUR_API_KEY
```

Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your actual Google Maps API key. The JSON response will contain details such as:

```json
{
  "results": [
    {
      "address_components": [...],
      "formatted_address": "1600 Amphitheatre Parkway, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA",
      "geometry": {
        "location": {
          "lat": 37.4224764,
          "lng": -122.0842499
        },
        ...
      },
      ...
    }
  ],
  "status": "OK"
}
```

That formatted address shows that the coordinates point to the Googleplex in Mountain View, California.

If you need help setting up the API key or parsing the response, just let me know!
